      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, we can use the relationship between the GCD, LCM, and the product of the numbers. The relationship is given by:\n\n\\[ \\text{GCD}(a, b) \\times \\text{LCM}(a, b) = a \\times b \\]\n\nHere, \\(a = 18\\) and \\(b = 42\\). First, we need to find the GCD and the LCM of 18 and 42.\n\n1. **Finding the GCD of 18 and 42:**\n   - The prime factorization of 18 is \\(2 \\times 3^2\\).\n   - The prime factorization of 42 is \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 7\\).\n   - The common factors are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\). The lowest power of these common factors is \\(2^1 \\times 3^1 = 6\\).\n   - Therefore, the GCD of 18 and 42 is 6.\n\n2. **Finding the LCM of 18 and 42:**\n   - The prime factorization of 18 is \\(2 \\times 3^2\\).\n   - The prime factorization of 42 is \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 7\\).\n   - The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations. The primes involved are 2, 3, and 7.\n     - The highest power of 2 is \\(2^1\\).\n     - The highest power of 3 is \\(3^2\\).\n     - The highest power of 7 is \\(7^1\\).\n   - Therefore, the LCM of 18 and 42 is \\(2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\\).\n\n3. **Calculating the product of the GCD and the LCM:**\n   - We have \\(\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 6\\) and \\(\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 126\\).\n   - The product is \\(6 \\times 126\\).\n   - To find \\(6 \\times 126\\), we can break it down:\n     \\[\n     6 \\times 126 = 6 \\times (120 + 6) = 6 \\times 120 + 6 \\times 6 = 720 + 36 = 756.\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
